  "prompt": "# Adversarial review \u2014 companion caplog control sub-codes (#417)\n\nTerse adversarial review. Offband MeshCore companion firmware. Bug: caplog capture couldn't be\nenabled on a companion (the #395 CLI verbs are in CommonCLI, which companion_radio doesn't use);\nthe companion 0xC4 command was download-only, so the primary #384 use case returned an empty\nbuffer. This adds request sub-codes to CMD_OFFBAND_CAPLOG (0xC4).\n\nContract added:\n- Request cmd_frame[1]: DOWNLOAD(0x01, default when len==1) / ENABLE(0x02,[level]) / DISABLE(0x03)\n  / ERASE(0x04) / STATUS(0x05).\n- Reply out_frame[1]: download stream START(0x01)/CHUNK(0x02)/END(0x03); control ACK(0x10)=\n  [0xC4,0x10,req_op,ok]; STATUS(0x11)=[0xC4,0x11,enabled,level,used(4B LE),cap(4B LE)].\n\nReview concretely (cite line, rank severity):\n1. Dispatch correctness: does the sub-code switch handle len==1 (bare 0xC4) as DOWNLOAD? Any\n   unknown-sub-code falling through to DOWNLOAD unintentionally (is that desired)? Frame lengths\n   passed to writeFrame correct for each reply?\n2. Response sub-code space: do the control replies (0x10/0x11) avoid colliding with the download\n   stream sub-codes (0x01/0x02/0x03) that a client parser switches on? Any ambiguity?\n3. State/interaction: ENABLE/DISABLE/ERASE while a download stream is in flight \u2014 any corruption?\n   Should control ops also be rejected during _caplog_streaming like DOWNLOAD is?\n4. STATUS frame: byte layout, endianness, out_frame bounds (out_frame is MAX_FRAME_SIZE+1).\n5. Anything missed \u2014 a security/DoS angle, the client contract, or a simpler approach.\n\nIf sound, say so.\n\n=== C:\\Users\\stryc\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Dev-meshcore-firmware\\ce5045af-5978-4a55-8b3c-9ce0975d4fe7\\scratchpad\\417-diff.txt ===\ndiff --git a/examples/companion_radio/MyMesh.cpp b/examples/companion_radio/MyMesh.cpp\nindex 711ee46e..ffc0baf7 100644\n--- a/examples/companion_radio/MyMesh.cpp\n+++ b/examples/companion_radio/MyMesh.cpp\n@@ -109,11 +109,22 @@ namespace offband { MqttBrokerPool& wifiObserverPool(); }\n // frame per idle main-loop pass (F8 #169 queue-safe). Sub-code in reply byte[1].\n // NOTE (#408): 0xC4, NOT 0xC3 -- 0xC3 is CMD_OFFBAND_FEM_LNA (OffbandConfigProtocol.h).\n // The 0xC-range allocation map lives in that header; keep new codes in sync there.\n-#define CMD_OFFBAND_CAPLOG            0xC4  // request: download the serial-capture buffer\n-#define RESP_CODE_OFFBAND_CAPLOG      0xC4  // reply: [0xC4, sub, ...]\n-#define CAPLOG_SUB_START              0x01  // [0xC4,0x01, total_len(4B LE)]\n-#define CAPLOG_SUB_CHUNK              0x02  // [0xC4,0x02, <up to MAX_FRAME_SIZE-2 bytes>]\n-#define CAPLOG_SUB_END                0x03  // [0xC4,0x03]\n+#define CMD_OFFBAND_CAPLOG            0xC4  // request: [0xC4, req_sub, args...]\n+#define RESP_CODE_OFFBAND_CAPLOG      0xC4  // reply:   [0xC4, resp_sub, ...]\n+// #417: request sub-code in cmd_frame[1] (absent/len==1 => DOWNLOAD, back-compat).\n+// Control ops let a COMPANION enable/erase/inspect capture (it has no CLI; the\n+// #395 CommonCLI verbs cover repeater/room-server only).\n+#define CAPLOG_REQ_DOWNLOAD           0x01  // [0xC4] or [0xC4,0x01]      -> START/CHUNK*/END\n+#define CAPLOG_REQ_ENABLE             0x02  // [0xC4,0x02,(level)]        -> ACK  (level: MLOG_*; default DEBUG)\n+#define CAPLOG_REQ_DISABLE            0x03  // [0xC4,0x03]                -> ACK\n+#define CAPLOG_REQ_ERASE              0x04  // [0xC4,0x04]                -> ACK\n+#define CAPLOG_REQ_STATUS             0x05  // [0xC4,0x05]                -> STATUS\n+// response sub-code in out_frame[1]:\n+#define CAPLOG_SUB_START              0x01  // download: [0xC4,0x01, total_len(4B LE)]\n+#define CAPLOG_SUB_CHUNK              0x02  // download: [0xC4,0x02, <up to MAX_FRAME_SIZE-2 bytes>]\n+#define CAPLOG_SUB_END                0x03  // download: [0xC4,0x03]\n+#define CAPLOG_RESP_ACK               0x10  // control:  [0xC4,0x10, req_op, ok(0|1)]\n+#define CAPLOG_RESP_STATUS            0x11  // status:   [0xC4,0x11, enabled, level, used(4B LE), cap(4B LE)]\n \n // Stats sub-types for CMD_GET_STATS\n #define STATS_TYPE_CORE               0\n@@ -1436,13 +1447,50 @@ void MyMesh::handleCmdFrame(size_t len) {\n     _serial->writeFrame(out_frame, 1 + strlen(txt) + 1);  // +1: NUL-terminated (Offband convention)\n     return;\n   }\n-  // #396: serial-capture download (companion-only). Freeze the ring (auto-stop\n-  // capture) so the offset stream is stable, send START with the total byte\n-  // count, then stream CHUNK* -> END one frame per idle pass via caplogDrain().\n+  // #396/#417: serial-capture (companion-only). cmd_frame[1] selects the op:\n+  // ENABLE/DISABLE/ERASE/STATUS are single-frame acks; DOWNLOAD (default) freezes\n+  // the ring and streams START -> CHUNK* -> END one frame per idle pass.\n   if (cmd_frame[0] == CMD_OFFBAND_CAPLOG) {\n-    // Reject if any streamed response is already in flight (self, block-list,\n-    // contacts, or observer config) \u2014 interleaving frames would corrupt the\n-    // client parser. Client sees RESP_CODE_ERR and can retry.\n+    uint8_t req = (len >= 2) ? cmd_frame[1] : CAPLOG_REQ_DOWNLOAD;\n+\n+    // --- #417 control ops: let a companion turn capture on/off/erase/inspect ---\n+    if (req == CAPLOG_REQ_ENABLE) {\n+      uint8_t level = (len >= 3) ? cmd_frame[2] : (uint8_t)MLOG_DEBUG;\n+      meshLogSetLevel(level);\n+      meshLogSetEnabled(true);\n+      out_frame[0] = RESP_CODE_OFFBAND_CAPLOG; out_frame[1] = CAPLOG_RESP_ACK;\n+      out_frame[2] = CAPLOG_REQ_ENABLE; out_frame[3] = 1;\n+      _serial->writeFrame(out_frame, 4);\n+      return;\n+    }\n+    if (req == CAPLOG_REQ_DISABLE) {\n+      meshLogSetEnabled(false);\n+      out_frame[0] = RESP_CODE_OFFBAND_CAPLOG; out_frame[1] = CAPLOG_RESP_ACK;\n+      out_frame[2] = CAPLOG_REQ_DISABLE; out_frame[3] = 1;\n+      _serial->writeFrame(out_frame, 4);\n+      return;\n+    }\n+    if (req == CAPLOG_REQ_ERASE) {\n+      meshLogClear();\n+      out_frame[0] = RESP_CODE_OFFBAND_CAPLOG; out_frame[1] = CAPLOG_RESP_ACK;\n+      out_frame[2] = CAPLOG_REQ_ERASE; out_frame[3] = 1;\n+      _serial->writeFrame(out_frame, 4);\n+      return;\n+    }\n+    if (req == CAPLOG_REQ_STATUS) {\n+      uint32_t used = (uint32_t)meshLogBytesUsed();\n+      uint32_t cap = (uint32_t)meshLogCapacity();\n+      out_frame[0] = RESP_CODE_OFFBAND_CAPLOG; out_frame[1] = CAPLOG_RESP_STATUS;\n+      out_frame[2] = meshLogIsEnabled() ? 1 : 0;\n+      out_frame[3] = meshLogGetLevel();\n+      memcpy(&out_frame[4], &used, 4);\n+      memcpy(&out_frame[8], &cap, 4);\n+      _serial->writeFrame(out_frame, 12);\n+      return;\n+    }\n+\n+    // --- DOWNLOAD (default): reject if any stream is already in flight, else\n+    // freeze the ring and start the START -> CHUNK* -> END stream. ---\n     if (_caplog_streaming || _blk_listing || _iter_started\n #ifdef OFFBAND_OBSERVER\n         || _ob_stream != OB_STREAM_NONE\n\n\n",
  "response": "Adversarial review complete. One moderate severity bug found; otherwise, the design is sound.\n\n### 1. Dispatch Correctness\n-   **`len==1` (bare `0xC4`)**: **Correct**. Line 1449's ternary `(len >= 2) ? ...` correctly assigns `CAPLOG_REQ_DOWNLOAD` to `req` when `len` is 1, maintaining backward compatibility.\n-   **Unknown sub-codes**: **Minor issue**. Any sub-code not matching `ENABLE` through `STATUS` will fall through to the download logic starting at L1480. This makes `DOWNLOAD` the default for any malformed request, which could be surprising. An explicit check for `req == CAPLOG_REQ_DOWNLOAD` and an error for other unknown codes would be more robust. This is not a bug but a point of design fragility.\n-   **Frame lengths**: **Correct**. All calls to `_serial->writeFrame()` use the correct length for the corresponding reply frame (`4` for ACKs, `12` for STATUS).\n\n### 2. Response Sub-code Space\n-   **Correct**. The download stream sub-codes (`0x01`-`0x03`) and control response sub-codes (`0x10`-`0x11`) occupy distinct, non-overlapping ranges. A client parser switching on `reply[1]` will not have any ambiguity.\n\n### 3. State/Interaction\n-   **Severity**: **Moderate**.\n-   **Bug**: The new control operations `ENABLE`, `DISABLE`, and `ERASE` can be invoked while a download stream is in progress (`_caplog_streaming == true`). The check that rejects new streams (L1480-1486) is positioned *after* the new control op handlers (L1452-1479).\n-   **Impact**: Calling `meshLogClear()` (for `ERASE` at L1466) while `caplogDrain()` is reading from the log buffer in the main loop could corrupt the stream, return partial data, or cause a crash (e.g., read-after-free). Similarly, `ENABLE`/`DISABLE` modify state the streaming process depends on.\n-   **Recommendation**: Reject state-changing control operations when a stream is active. The `STATUS` query is read-only and likely safe, but for consistency, all control ops except `DOWNLOAD` could be gated by the same check.\n\n    ```cpp\n    // In MyMesh::handleCmdFrame, inside `if (cmd_frame[0] == CMD_OFFBAND_CAPLOG)`\n    if (req != CAPLOG_REQ_DOWNLOAD && (_caplog_streaming || _blk_listing || ...)) {\n        // Send RESP_CODE_ERR, this is a busy/conflicting operation\n        return;\n    }\n    ```\n\n### 4. STATUS frame\n-   **Byte layout**: **Correct**. The frame structure `[code, sub, enabled, level, used(4), cap(4)]` matches the implementation at L1473-1477 and the final size of 12 bytes at L1478.\n-   **Endianness**: **Minor issue**. Lines 1476-1477 use `memcpy` to write the 32-bit `used` and `cap` values. This implicitly relies on the host MCU being Little-Endian to match the contract (`LE`). This is common for target hardware but is not portable. Explicitly serializing the bytes would be more robust.\n-   **Bounds**: **Correct**. A 12-byte reply is well within `out_frame`'s capacity.\n\n### 5. Anything Missed\n-   No security or DoS issues of note were found beyond what is typical for an unauthenticated serial protocol.\n-   The design could be slightly cleaner using a `switch` statement on `req`, which would make handling of the default/unknown case more explicit.\n\nThe change is not sound as-is due to the state corruption issue (point 3)."
